ot be factored into smaller integers, it must be included entirely in either \\( p_1 \\) or \\( p_2 \\).\n\n2. **Divisibility Constraint**:\n   - To ensure \\( \\frac{p_1}{p_2} \\) is minimized, the prime nature of \\( 7 \\) suggests it should be in \\( p_1 \\) because it would not be divisible by any other group split without \\( 7 \\).\n   - Therefore, \\( \\frac{p_1}{p_2} \\geq 7 \\).\n\n3. **Optimal Group Split**: \n   We split the numbers such that \\( p_1 \\) and \\( p_2 \\) are as balanced as possible while following the above condition.\n   \n4. **Trial Grouping**:\n   Let\u2019s test with the smaller products:\n   - \\( p_1 = 3 \\times 5 \\times 6 \\times 7 \\times 8 \\), i.e., \\( p_1 = 3 \\times 5 \\times (2 \\times 3) \\times 7 \\times (2 \\times 4) = 3 \\times 5 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 7 \\times 2 \\times 4 \\),\n   - \\( p_2 = 1 \\times 2 \\times 4 \\times 9 \\times 10 \\), i.e., \\( p_2 = 1 \\times 2 \\times (2 \\times 2) \\times (3 \\times 3) \\times (2 \\times 5) \\).\n\n   Now compute:\n   \\[\n   p_1 = 3 \\times 5 \\times 6 \\times 7 \\times 8 = 3 \\times 5 \\times (2 \\times 3) \\times 7 \\times (2 \\times 4) = 3 \\times 5 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 7 \\times 2 \\times 4 = 3 \\times 3 \\times 5 \\times 7 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\times 4\n   \\]\n   Thus, \\( p_1 = 5040 \\).\n\n   Now compute for \\( p_2 \\):\n   \\[\n   p_2 = 1 \\times 2 \\times 4 \\times 9 \\times 10 = 1 \\times 2 \\times (2 \\times 2) \\times (3 \\times 3) \\times (2 \\times 5) = 2^3 \\times 3^2 \\times 5 \\times 10 = 2^3 \\times 3^2 \\times 5 = 720\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the Ratio**:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{p_1}{p_2} = \\frac{5040}{720} = 7\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThus, the minimum value of \\( \\frac{p_1}{p_2} \\) is\n\\(\\boxed{7}\\).\n```", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In a triangle with sides \\(a, b, c\\) and angles \\(\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma\\), the equality \\(3 \\alpha + 2 \\beta = 180^{\\circ}\