btract the given equation from the angle sum equation:\n   \\[\n   \\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3\\alpha + 2\\beta = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Subtract the first equation from the second:\n   \\[\n   (3\\alpha + 2\\beta) - (\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma) = 180^\\circ - 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Simplify:\n   \\[\n   2\\alpha + \\beta - \\gamma = 0\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\gamma = 2\\alpha + \\beta\n   \\]\n\n3. We also know that the sides opposite these angles are $a$, $b$, and $c$, respectively, with $a = 2$ and $b = 3$.\n\n4. Construct point $D$ on $AB$ such that $AD = AC$. Therefore, triangle $ACD$ is isosceles with $\\angle ACD = \\angle ADC = 90^\\circ - \\frac{\\alpha}{2}$.\n\n5. Next, we analyze triangle $CBD$. Since $\\angle BCD = \\alpha$, and $\\angle CBD = 180^\\circ - \\angle BDC - \\beta = 180^\\circ - (90^\\circ - \\frac{\\alpha}{2}) - \\beta$, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\angle BCD + \\beta = \\angle ADC\n   \\]\n   So:\n   \\[\n   \\angle ADC = 90^\\circ - \\frac{\\alpha}{2} = \\alpha + \\beta\n   \\]\n   Hence:\n   \\[\n   \\angle BCD = \\alpha\n   \\]\n   Consequently, triangles $\\triangle CBD$ and $\\triangle ABC$ are similar by the AA (Angle-Angle) criterion.\n\n6. Because the triangles are similar:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BD}{BC} = \\frac{BC}{AB} \\implies \\frac{c - b}{a} = \\frac{a}{c}\n   \\]\n   Substitute $a = 2$ and $b = 3$:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{c - 3}{2} = \\frac{2}{c}\n   \\]\n\n7. Cross-multiplying gives:\n   \\[\n   (c - 3)c = 4 \\implies c^2 - 3c - 4 = 0\n   \\]\n\n8. Solve the quadratic equation:\n   \\[\n   c^2 - 3c - 4 = 0\n   \\]\n   Use the quadratic formula $c = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$ where $a = 1$, $b = -3$, and $c = -4$:\n   \\[\n   c = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 + 16}}{2} \\implies c = \\frac{3 \\pm 5}{2}\n   \\]\n\n9. Hence, the solutions are:\n   \\[\n   c = \\frac{3 + 5}{2} = 4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad c = \\frac{3 - 5}{2} = -1\n   \\]\n   Since a side length cannot be negative:\n   \\[\n