\n4. **Apply the transformation logic:**\n   The transformation from \\(a x^2 + b x + c > 0\\) to \\(c x^2 - b x + a > 0\\) involves reversing the roles of the coefficients \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) and their corresponding coefficients in the quadratic equation. \n\n5. **Find the roots from the complementary inequalities:**\n   Since we had the intervals where \\(a x^2 + b x + c > 0\\) were obtained, we must invert the intervals symmetrically for \\(c x^2 - b x + a > 0\\). This is indicative of the polynomial having roots outside of the \\((-3, 4)\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n   By reinterpretating the intervals and knowing quadratic properties, it can be concluded that the solution for \\(c x^2 - b x + a > 0\\) will logically be the intervals where the solution set is outside of the roots \\(-3\\) and \\(4\\):\n\n\\[ \n\\left( - \\infty, - \\frac{1}{4} \\right) \\cup \\left( \\frac{1}{3}, + \\infty \\right)\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution set for \\(c x^2 - b x + a > 0\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left( - \\infty, - \\frac{1}{4} \\right) \\cup \\left( \\frac{1}{3}, + \\infty \\right)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the minimum real number \\( A \\) such that for every quadratic polynomial \\( f(x) \\) satisfying the condition \\( |f(x)| \\leq 1 \\) for \\( 0 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\), the inequality \\( f^{\\prime}(0) \\leq A \\) holds.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the minimum real number A such that for every quadratic polynomial f(x) that satisfies |f(x)| \u2264 1 on the interval [0, 1], the derivative at 0, f\u2019(0), is \u2264 A. Hmm. Let me start by understanding the problem step by step.\n\nFirst, since f(x) is a quadratic polynomial, it can be written in the general form: f(x) = ax\u00b2 + bx + c, where a, b, c are real coefficients. The derivative f\u2019(x) would then be 2ax + b, so f\u2019(0) is just b. Therefore, the problem is essentially asking for the maximum possi