, no. If Petya's reversed video is 5 minutes long, starting at school, then at time t, he has walked t minutes towards home. So the remaining time in Petya's reversed video is (5 - t) minutes. But since they splice at the meeting point, do they include that remaining part?\n\nWait, maybe the total duration is t + (5 - t) = 5 minutes? But that doesn't make sense. Wait, no. Let's think.\n\nThe original Vasya's video is 8 minutes. They are editing by playing Vasya's video from the start and Petya's reversed video from the start (which is the end of Petya's original video). They play both simultaneously until they meet at time t. At that point, they cut Vasya's video and splice in Petya's video from that point. But Petya's reversed video is already being played from the start (school) backwards. So the spliced video would be the first t minutes of Vasya walking to school, then the remaining (5 - t) minutes of Petya's reversed video walking back home. Wait, but Petya's reversed video is 5 minutes total. If they have played t minutes of it, then the remaining is 5 - t minutes. So the total duration would be t + (5 - t) = 5 minutes. But Vasya's original video is 8 minutes. But that would make the total video 5 minutes, but that doesn't account for Vasya's part.\n\nWait, perhaps not. Let me visualize:\n\n- Vasya's video: 0 to 8 minutes, moving from home to school.\n\n- Petya's reversed video: 0 to 5 minutes, moving from school to home.\n\nThey start both videos at time 0. At time t, Vasya is at position (D/8)t, Petya is at position D - (D/5)t.\n\nThey meet when (D/8)t = D - (D/5)t. Solving for t gives t = 40/13 \u2248 3.0769 minutes.\n\nAt that point, Vasya's video has been playing for 40/13 minutes, and Petya's reversed video has also been playing for 40/13 minutes. Since Petya's reversed video is 5 minutes long, the remaining time in Petya's video is 5 - 40/13 = (65 - 40)/13 = 25/13 \u2248 1.923 minutes.\n\nBut when they splice, do they continue playing the remaining Pety