ol, 8 minutes).\n\n- Start Petya's video from the end, reversed (so it's school to home, 5 minutes).\n\nPlay both videos simultaneously. When their positions coincide, splice Petya's video into Vasya's.\n\nThe resulting video is Vasya walking from home until the meeting point, then Petya walking backward from the meeting point to home.\n\nTherefore, the duration of the resulting video is the time from home to meeting point in Vasya's video plus the time from meeting point to home in Petya's reversed video.\n\nBut Vasya's time to the meeting point is t = 40/13 minutes.\n\nPetya's reversed video is moving from school to home at his original speed (D/5 per minute). The distance from the meeting point to home is (D/8)*t, since that's where Vasya was. Alternatively, since Petya starts at school and moves towards home, the distance from meeting point to home is D - (D - (D/5)*t) = (D/5)*t. Wait, no. Let me calculate the distance from meeting point to home.\n\nVasya's position at meeting time t is (D/8)*t. So the distance from home is (D/8)*t, which is the same as Petya's position from school: D - (D/5)*t. Wait, since they meet at the same point, the distance from home is (D/8)*t, and the distance from school is D - (D/5)*t. But since they are the same point, (D/8)*t = D - (D/5)*t, which we already solved.\n\nSo the distance from the meeting point to home is (D/8)*t. In Petya's reversed video, he is moving from school to home at speed D/5 per minute. So the time needed to cover the distance from meeting point to home is distance divided by speed: [(D/8)*t] / (D/5) = (5/8)*t.\n\nBut t is 40/13 minutes. So the time would be (5/8)*(40/13) = (200/104) = 25/13 \u2248 1.923 minutes.\n\nTherefore, total duration is t + (5/8)t = t*(1 + 5/8) = t*(13/8). But t is 40/13, so (40/13)*(13/8) = 40/8 = 5 minutes.\n\nWait, that's interesting. So the total duration is 5 minutes. That makes sense because Petya's reversed video is 5 minutes long, and they are splicing it in such a way that the overlapping part is accounted for. But why does it come out exactly to 5 minutes? Because when you combine the two movements, their relative speeds add up, and the meeting time is calculated, but then the remaining time from Petya's side gives the total as his full reversed video duration.\n\nAlternatively, since Vasya and Petya are moving towards each other in the edited video (Vasya forward, Petya backward), their relative speed is (D/8 + D/5). The total distance between them is D (since Vasya starts at home and Petya starts at school). So time to meet is D / (D/8 + D/5) = 1 / (1/8 + 1/5) = 1 / (13/40) = 40/13 minutes. Then, the remaining distance for Petya to home is D - (D/5)*(40/13) = D - (8D/13) = (5D/13). Time to cover that at speed D/5 is (5D/13)/(D/5) = 25/13 minutes. Total time 40/13 + 25/13 = 65/13 = 5 minutes.\n\nYes, that's the same as before. So the total duration is 5 minutes. But why does this happen? Because effectively, the combined movement covers the entire distance D in 5 minutes, which is Petya's original time. But Vasya's time was longer. However, since they are moving towards each other, the time is less. But the result here is that the total duration equals Petya's original video time. Is there a simpler way to see this?\n\nAlternatively, think of it as a relative speed problem. Vasya's speed is D/8, Petya's speed in reverse is D/5. They are moving towards each other with combined speed D/8 + D/5 = (5D + 8D)/40 = 13D/40 per minute. The distance between them is D, so time to meet is D / (13D/40) = 40/13 minutes. After meeting, the remaining part of Petya's reversed video is 5 - 40/13 = 25/13 minutes. But adding them gives 5 minutes. So total duration is 5 minutes.\n\nWait, but why is the remaining part of Petya's video 5 - t? Because Petya's reversed video is 5 minutes long. If they played t minutes of it, then there's 5 - t minutes left. So total duration is t + (5 - t) = 5 minutes. That's why it's exactly Petya's video length. Because the meeting happens during the overlapping playback of both videos, and after the splice, the remaining Petya's reversed video is appended, making the total duration 5 minutes.\n\nAlternatively, imagine that both videos are played simultaneously. Vasya's 8-minute video and Petya's 5-minute reversed video. They start at the same time, and the video ends when either of the videos ends. But since they splice at the meeting point, the resulting video duration is the minimum of the two times? No, because in this case, the meeting time is 40/13 \u2248 3.08 minutes, which is less than both 5 and 8 minutes. But the total duration after splicing becomes 5 minutes. So it's not just the meeting time.\n\nWait, perhaps another approach. The spliced video consists of two parts:\n\n1. Vasya's video from start until meeting time t.\n\n2. Petya's reversed video from meeting time t until the end.\n\nThe first part's duration is t. The second part's duration is the remaining time of Petya's reversed video after t, which is 5 - t. So total duration is t + (5 - t) = 5 minutes.\n\nAh, that's it! Because Petya's reversed video is 5 minutes long. When you take t minutes from the start (which is the end of his original video), the remaining part is 5 - t minutes of the reversed video, which corresponds to the beginning part of his original video (home to meeting point) played in reverse. Therefore, the total duration is 5 minutes.\n\nSo regardless of when they meet, the total duration would be the length of Petya's reversed video, which is 5 minutes. But that seems counterintuitive. Wait, no. Because if the meeting time were to happen after Petya's video has already ended, then the total duration would be different. But in this case, since the meeting time occurs before both videos end (t = 40/13 \u2248 3.08 < 5 and <8), the remaining part of Petya's video is 5 - t, and adding to t gives 5. So the total duration is exactly the length of Petya's reversed video.\n\nBut why is that the case? Because the splice happens at the meeting point, and after that, they continue showing Petya's reversed video from that point until the end. But since Petya's reversed video started at the same time as Vasya's, the remaining time after the splice is 5 - t. Therefore, total duration is t + (5 - t) = 5. So the answer is 5 minutes.\n\nBut let's verify with numbers. Suppose D = 40 units (to make calculations easy).\n\nVasya's speed: 40/8 = 5 units per minute.\n\nPetya's speed: 40/5 = 8 units per minute.\n\nIn the edited video:\n\nVasya starts at home (0) moving forward at 5 units/min.\n\nPetya starts at school (40) moving backward at 8 units/min.\n\nTime to meet: t.\n\nPosition of Vasya: 5t.\n\nPosition of Petya: 40 - 8t.\n\nSet equal: 5t = 40 -8t \u2192 13t =40 \u2192 t=40/13 \u22483.08.\n\nAt that time, Vasya has walked 5*(40/13)=200/13 \u224815.38 units.\n\nPetya has walked 8*(40/13)=320/13 \u224824.62 units from school, so position is 40 -24.62\u224815.38 units. Correct.\n\nNow, after splicing, the video shows Vasya up to 15.38 units (t=40/13), then switches to Petya's reversed video. Petya's reversed video started at school (40) and was moving backward. At t=40/13, Petya is at 15.38 units. The remaining distance to home is15.38 units. Petya's speed is 8 units/min, so time to home is15.38 /8 = (200/13)/8=25/13\u22481.92 minutes.\n\nTotal duration:40/13 +25/13=65/13=5 minutes.\n\nYes, exactly 5 minutes. Therefore, the duration of the resulting video is 5 minutes.", "deepseek_solution": "The duration of the resulting video is determined by the time it takes for Vasya and Petya's videos to meet at the same point, plus the remaining time from Petya's reversed video after the splice. Here's the breakdown:\n\n1. **Define Variables**:\n   - Let \\( D \\) be the total distance from home to school.\n   - Vasya's speed: \\( \\frac{D}{8} \\) per minute (takes 8 minutes).\n   - Petya's speed (reversed): \\( \\frac{D}{5} \\) per minute (original 5-minute video played backward).\n\n2. **Meeting Point Calculation**:\n   - Vasya's position at time \\( t \\): \\( \\frac{D}{8}t \\).\n   - Petya's reversed position at time \\( t \\): \\( D - \\frac{D}{5}t \\).\n   - Set positions equal:  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{D}{8}t = D - \\frac{D}{5}t.\n     \\]\n   - Solve for \\( t \\):  \n     \\[\n     t = \\frac{40}{13} \\approx 3.08 \\text{ minutes}.\n     \\]\n\n3. **Remaining Time in Petya's Reversed Video**:\n   - Petya's reversed video is 5 minutes long. After \\( t = \\frac{40}{13} \\) minutes, the remaining time is:  \n     \\[\n     5 - \\frac{40}{13} = \\frac{25}{13} \\approx 1.92 \\text{ minutes}.\n     \\]\n\n4. **Total Duration**:\n   - The resulting video combines the time until the meeting (\\( t \\)) and the remaining Petya footage:  \n     \\[\n     \\frac{40}{13} + \\frac{25}{13} = \\frac{65}{13} = 5 \\text{ minutes}.\n     \\]\n\n**Answer**: The duration of the resulting video is \\(\\boxed{5}\\) minutes.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Understanding the components of the video**: \n   We start with Vasya's video showing him walking to school, which lasts 8 minutes. Then we switch to Petya's video, which is played in reverse, showing him walking back home from school in 5 minutes. \n\n2. **Visualizing the synchronization point**: \n    Let's consider the point in time when Vasya and Petya are at the same point on their respective paths. This point marks the transition from Vasya's video to Petya's reverse video. \n\n3. **Evaluating the combined video length**: \n    - Between this synchronization point and Vasya\u2019s starting point, the entire segment of Vasya\u2019s walking has 8 minutes duration.\n    - From this synchronization point to Petya\u2019s end point (which is his home), Petya's reverse walking video adds up to 5 minutes.\n\n4. **Transition point and maintaining synchronous progress**:\n    - When combining these two videos, the actual total time does not account for double-counting since once they meet, we consider transitioning to Petya's reverse walk. Essentially, after they meet, the subsequent part follows seamlessly showing Petya's reverse journey.\n\n5. **Determining the final length**:\n    Given that both videos will together cover the entire path from Vasya starting at home, reaching school and then from the synchronized point, Petya moves away to home (in reverse visualization), we only need to consider one continuous path.\n    \n    Hence, the final duration of the full combined video will be equivalent to Petya\u2019s journey from meeting point back home, which is:\n \n\\[\n\\text{Duration of the completed video} = 5 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{5 \\text{ minutes}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nx^{2} + y^{2} + 6xy = 68 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 2y^{2} - 3xy = 16\n\\end{gathered}\n$$", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this system of equations to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nx^{2} + y^{2} + 6xy = 68 \\quad (1) \\\\\n2x^{2} + 2y^{2} - 3xy = 16 \\quad (2)\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nHmm, let me think about how to approach this. Both equations are quadratic in x and y. Maybe I can manipulate them to eliminate some terms or find a substitution. Let me see.\n\nFirst, notice that equation (1) and equation (2) both have terms with \\(x^2 + y^2\\). Maybe I can express \\(x^2 + y^2\\) from one equation and substitute into the other. Let me try that.\n\nLet me denote \\(S = x + y\\) and \\(P = xy\\). Sometimes, in symmetric equations, this substitution helps. But let me check if that's applicable here.\n\nWait, equation (1) is \\(x^2 + y^2 + 6xy = 68\\). Since \\(x^2 + y^2 = (x + y)^2 - 2xy = S^2 - 2P\\), substituting that into equation (1) gives:\n\n\\(S^2 - 2P + 6P = 68\\)  \nSimplify: \\(S^2 + 4P = 68\\). Let me write that as equation (1a):\n\n\\(S^2 + 4P = 68 \\quad (1a)\\)\n\nNow, equation (2) is \\(2x^2 + 2y^2 - 3xy = 16\\). Factor out the 2:\n\n\\(2(x^2 + y^2) - 3xy = 16\\). Again, \\(x^2 + y^2 = S^2 - 2P\\), so substitute:\n\n\\(2(S^2 - 2P) - 3P = 16\\)  \nExpand: \\(2S^2 - 4P - 3P = 16\\)  \nCombine like terms: \\(2S^2 - 7P = 16\\). Let's call this equation (2a):\n\n\\(2S^2 - 7P = 16 \\quad (2a)\\)\n\nNow, we have a system of two equations with variables S and P:\n\n1. \\(S^2 + 4P = 68\\)  \n2. \\(2S^2 - 7P = 16\\)\n\nThis seems more manageable. Let me solve this system. Let me denote equation (1a) and (2a). I can solve for one variable in terms of the other. Let's solve equation (1a) for P:\n\nFrom (1a): \\(4P = 68 - S^2\\)  \nSo, \\(P = \\frac{68 - S^2}{4}\\)  \n\nNow, substitute this expression for P into equation (2a):\n\n\\(2S^2 - 7\\left(\\frac{68 - S^2}{4}\\right) = 16\\)\n\nMultiply through by 4 to eliminate the denominator:\n\n\\(8S^2 - 7(68 - S^2) = 64\\)\n\nExpand the terms:\n\n\\(8S^2 - 476 + 7S^2 = 64\\)\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\(15S^2 - 476 = 64\\)\n\nAdd 476 to both sides:\n\n\\(15S^2 = 64 + 476 = 540\\)\n\nDivide both sides by 15:\n\n\\(S^2 = 540 / 15 = 36\\)\n\nSo, \\(S^2 = 36\\), which means \\(S = 6\\) or \\(S = -6\\).\n\nNow, let's find P for each case.\n\nCase 1: \\(S = 6\\)\n\nFrom equation (1a): \\(6^2 + 4P = 68\\)  \n36 + 4P = 68  \n4P = 68 - 36 = 32  \nP = 8\n\nCase 2: \\(S = -6\\)\n\nFrom equation (1a): \\((-6)^2 + 4P = 68\\)  \n36 + 4P = 68  \nSame as above: 4P = 32 \u2192 P = 8\n\nSo, regardless of S being 6 or -6, P is 8. Wait, that's interesting. So both cases give the same P. Hmm. So S is either 6 or -6, and P is 8 in both cases.\n\nTherefore, we have two possibilities:\n\n1. \\(x + y = 6\\) and \\(xy = 8\\)\n2. \\(x + y = -6\\) and \\(xy = 8\\)\n\nNow, let's analyze each case.\n\nFirst, Case 1: \\(x + y = 6\\), \\(xy = 8\\)\n\nThis is a system of equations that can be solved by forming a quadratic equation. If x and y are roots of the equation \\(t^2 - St + P = 0\\), then:\n\nFor S = 6, P = 8:\n\n\\(t^2 - 6t + 8 = 0\\)\n\nSolving this quadratic equation:\n\nDiscriminant D = 36 - 32 = 4\n\nRoots:\n\n\\(t = [6 \\pm \\sqrt{4}]/2 = [6 \\pm 2]/2\\)\n\nThus:\n\nt = (6 + 2)/2 = 8/2 = 4  \nt = (6 - 2)/2 = 4/2 = 2\n\nSo solutions are (x, y) = (4, 2) and (2, 4)\n\nCase 2: \\(x + y = -6\\), \\(xy = 8\\)\n\nSimilarly, forming the quadratic equation:\n\n\\(t^2 - (-6)t + 8 = t^2 + 6t + 8 = 0\\)\n\nSolving this:\n\nDiscriminant D = 36 - 32 = 4\n\nRoots:\n\n\\(t = [-6 \\pm \\sqrt{4}]/2 = [-6 \\pm 2]/2\\)\n\nThus:\n\nt = (-6 + 2)/2 = -4/2 = -2  \nt = (-6 - 2)/2 = -8/2 = -4\n\nSo solutions are (x, y) = (-2, -4) and (-4, -2)\n\nTherefore, the system has four solutions: (4,2), (2,4), (-2,-4), (-4,-2)\n\nWait, but I need to verify if all these solutions satisfy the original equations. Sometimes when substituting, especially with quadratic systems, extraneous solutions can be introduced. Let me check each solution in both equations.\n\nFirst, check (4,2):\n\nEquation (1): \\(4^2 + 2^2 + 6*4*2 = 16 + 4 + 48 = 68 \u2713\\)  \nEquation (2): \\(2*16 + 2*4 - 3*4*2 = 32 + 8 - 24 = 16 \u2713\\)\n\n(2,4):\n\nEquation (1): same as above, since it's symmetric: 68 \u2713  \nEquation (2): same as above: 16 \u2713\n\nNow (-2, -4):\n\nEquation (1): (-2)^2 + (-4)^2 + 6*(-2)*(-4) = 4 + 16 + 48 = 68 \u2713  \nEquation (2): 2*(4) + 2*(16) - 3*(-2)*(-4) = 8 + 32 - 24 = 16 \u2713\n\n(-4, -2):\n\nSame as above: 68 and 16 \u2713\n\nSo all four solutions are valid. Therefore, the solutions are indeed (4,2), (2,4), (-2,-4), (-4,-2).\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way to approach the original equations. Let me see if I can cross-verify.\n\nAnother approach: Let me try to subtract or combine the original equations. Let's see.\n\nEquation (1): \\(x^2 + y^2 + 6xy = 68\\)\n\nEquation (2): \\(2x^2 + 2y^2 - 3xy = 16\\)\n\nMaybe multiply equation (1) by 2 to make coefficients of x^2 + y^2 match:\n\nEquation (1)*2: \\(2x^2 + 2y^2 + 12xy = 136\\)  \nEquation (2): \\(2x^2 + 2y^2 - 3xy = 16\\)\n\nNow subtract equation (2) from equation (1)*2:\n\n(2x\u00b2 + 2y\u00b2 +12xy) - (2x\u00b2 +2y\u00b2 -3xy) = 136 -16  \nWhich gives: 15xy = 120  \nTherefore: xy = 8\n\nAh, so that's another way to get xy = 8 directly. Then, knowing that xy =8, substitute back into one of the equations.\n\nFrom equation (2): 2x\u00b2 + 2y\u00b2 -3xy =16. Since xy=8, then:\n\n2x\u00b2 + 2y\u00b2 - 24 =16  \nThus, 2x\u00b2 + 2y\u00b2 = 40  \nDivide by 2: x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 =20\n\nSo now, we have:\n\nx\u00b2 + y\u00b2 =20  \nxy=8\n\nThen, similar to earlier approach, using x + y = S and x y = P. Since x\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = (x + y)^2 - 2xy = S\u00b2 - 16 =20  \nTherefore, S\u00b2 =20 +16=36  \nThus, S=6 or S=-6, leading to same cases as before. So same solutions.\n\nSo that's another way to get to the same result. So either way, the solutions are the same. Therefore, the four solutions are correct.\n\nSo I think that's thorough. The key was to manipulate the equations to express in terms of S and P or directly find xy and then proceed. Both approaches lead to the same solutions. Verified all solutions and they satisfy the original equations.", "deepseek_solution": "To solve the system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nx^{2} + y^{2} + 6xy = 68 \\quad (1) \\\\\n2x^{2} + 2y^{2} - 3xy = 16 \\quad (2)\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\n**Step 1: Simplify the equations**  \nMultiply equation (1) by 2 to align coefficients with equation (2):  \n$$2x^{2} + 2y^{2} + 12xy = 136 \\quad (1')$$  \n\nSubtract equation (2) from (1'):  \n$$(2x^{2} + 2y^{2} + 12xy) - (2x^{2} + 2y^{2} - 3xy) = 136 - 16$$  \n$$15xy = 120 \\implies xy = 8$$  \n\n**Step 2: Substitute \\(xy = 8\\) into equation (2)**  \n$$2x^{2} + 2y^{2} - 3(8) = 16$$  \n$$2(x^{2} + y^{2}) = 40 \\implies x^{2} + y^{2} = 20$$  \n\n**Step 3: Solve for \\(x + y\\)**  \nUse the identity \\(x^{2} + y^{2} = (x + y)^2 - 2xy\\):  \n$$20 = (x + y)^2 - 16 \\implies (x + y)^2 = 36$$  \n$$\\implies x + y = 6 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x + y = -6$$  \n\n**Step 4: Solve quadratic systems**  \nFor \\(x + y = 6\\) and \\(xy = 8\\):  \n$$t^2 - 6t + 8 = 0 \\implies t = 4, 2$$  \nSolutions: \\((4, 2)\\) and \\((2, 4)\\).  \n\nFor \\(x + y = -6\\) and \\(xy = 8\\):  \n$$t^2 + 6t + 8 = 0 \\implies t = -2, -4$$  \nSolutions: \\((-2, -4)\\) and \\((-4, -2)\\).  \n\n**Verification**: All solutions satisfy the original equations.\n\n**Final Solutions**:  \n$$\\boxed{(4, 2), \\ (2, 4), \\ (-2, -4), \\ (-4, -2)}$$", "ground_truth_solution": "\nGiven the system of equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx^2 + y^2 + 6xy = 68 \\\\\n2x^2 + 2y^2 - 3xy = 16\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n1. **Express the equations in a simplified form:**\n\n   The first equation is:\n\n   \\[\n   x^2 + y^2 + 6xy = 68 \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The second equation is:\n\n   \\[\n   2x^2 + 2y^2 - 3xy = 16 \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Eliminate \\(x^2\\) and \\(y^2\\):**\n\n   Multiply (1) by 2:\n\n   \\[\n   2(x^2 + y^2 + 6xy) = 2 \\cdot 68 \n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying, we get:\n\n   \\[\n   2x^2 + 2y^2 + 12xy = 136 \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Subtract equation (2) from equation (3):**\n\n   \\[\n   (2x^2 + 2y^2 + 12xy) - (2x^2 + 2y^2 - 3xy) = 136 - 16\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying the left-hand side:\n\n   \\[\n   2x^2 + 2y^2 + 12xy - 2x^2 - 2y^2 + 3xy = 120\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   15xy = 120\n   \\]\n\n   Solving for \\(xy\\):\n\n   \\[\n   xy = \\frac{120}{15} = 8\n   \\]\n\n   So, the product of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) is:\n\n   \\[\n   xy = 8 \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Use \\(xy = 8\\) to find potential values for \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):**\n\n   Substituting \\(xy = 8\\) back into the equations, we solve for \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   x^2 + y^2 + 6 \\cdot 8 = 68 \\\\\n   2x^2 + 2y^2 - 3 \\cdot 8 = 16\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   For the first equation, substitute \\(xy = 8\\):\n\n   \\[\n   x^2 + y^2 + 48 = 68\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying:\n\n   \\[\n   x^2 + y^2 = 20 \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   For the second equation, substitute \\(xy = 8\\):\n\n   \\[\n   2x^2 + 2y^2 - 24 = 16\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying:\n\n   \\[\n   2x^2 + 2y^2 = 40\n   \\]\n\n   Dividing by 2:\n\n   \\[\n   x^2 + y^2 = 20\n   \\]\n\n   Which agrees with \\((5)\\).\n\n5. **Solve for \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):**\n\n   We have the system:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   x^2 + y^2 = 20 \\\\\n   xy = 8\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   Let \\(t = x + y\\) and \\(p = xy\\). Using these transformations:\n\n   \\[\n   t^2 = x^2 + y^2 + 2xy\n   \\]\n\n   Substitute known values:\n\n   \\[\n   t^2 = 20 + 2 \\cdot 8\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   t^2 = 20 + 16 = 36\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   t = \\pm 6\n   \\]\n\n   Given \\(t^2 = 36\\):\n   \n   \\[\n   x + y = 6 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x + y = -6\n   \\]\n\n   Solve these quadratic equations:\n\n   \\[\n   x^2 - (x+y)x + xy = 0 \\Rightarrow x^2 - 6x + 8 = 0\n   \\]\n\n   Solving by the quadratic formula:\n\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{6^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 8}}{2 \\cdot 1}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 - 32}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{4}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{6 \\pm 2}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   x = 4 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = 2 \n   \\]\n\n   Corresponding \\(y\\) values:\n\n   \\[\n   y = 2 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad y = 4\n   \\]\n\n   And for the case \\(x + y = -6\\):\n\n   Similarly, \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) values will be \\(-4, -2\\).\n\nConclusion:\nThe solutions are:\n\n\\[\n(x, y) = (4, 2), (2, 4), (-4, -2), (-2, -4)\n\\]\n\nHence:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(4, 2), (2, 4), (-4, -2), (-2, -4)}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Prove that a polynomial \\( P(z) \\) is an even function of \\( z \\in \\mathbf{C} \\) if and only if there exists a polynomial \\( Q(z) \\) such that \\( P(z) \\equiv Q(z) \\cdot Q(-z), z \\in \\mathbf{C} \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that a polynomial P(z) is even if and only if there exists another polynomial Q(z) such that P(z) = Q(z) * Q(-z) for all complex z. Hmm, let's break this down. First, let me recall what an even function is. An even function satisfies P(z) = P(-z) for all z in its domain, which here is the complex plane. So the problem is stating that P is even exactly when it can be factored as the product of Q(z) and Q(-z) for some polynomial Q.\n\nLet me start with the \"if\" direction. Suppose there exists a polynomial Q(z) such that P(z) = Q(z) * Q(-z). Then, to check if P is even, I need to verify that P(-z) = P(z). Let's compute P(-z):\n\nP(-z) = Q(-z) * Q(-(-z)) = Q(-z) * Q(z) = Q(z) * Q(-z) = P(z). \n\nSo that works out. Therefore, if P can be written as Q(z)Q(-z), then P is even. That direction seems straightforward.\n\nNow for the converse: if P is even, then there exists a polynomial Q such that P(z) = Q(z)Q(-z). This seems trickier. Let me think about how to construct Q(z) from P(z). Since P is even, all its terms have even degrees. So, if P is a polynomial, it can be written as P(z) = a_0 + a_2 z^2 + a_4 z^4 + ... + a_{2n} z^{2n}. \n\nBut how does that help me factor it into Q(z)Q(-z)? Maybe if I can write Q(z) as a polynomial whose coefficients are related to those of P(z). Let's suppose Q(z) is a polynomial of degree m, then Q(-z) is also a polynomial of degree m, so their product would be a polynomial of degree 2m. Therefore, the degree of P must be even, which makes sense because if P is even, all its terms are even degree, so its degree is even. So P has even degree, say 2m.\n\nSo perhaps Q(z) is a polynomial of degree m such that when multiplied by Q(-z), it gives P(z). Let me try to think in terms of roots. If P(z) is even, then whenever a is a root, so is -a. Moreover, since we are dealing with complex polynomials, the roots come in pairs {a, -a}. However, in the complex plane, roots don't necessarily come in such pairs unless the polynomial is even. Wait, but since P(z) is even, P(a) = 0 implies P(-a) = 0. So the roots are symmetric with respect to the origin.\n\nBut in the complex plane, roots can be anywhere, so unless there's a root at 0, all roots come in pairs {a, -a}. If there is a root at 0, since P is even, its multiplicity must be even. Therefore, the polynomial P can be written as P(z) = z^{2k} * product_{i=1}^m (z - a_i)(z + a_i) where each a_i is a non-zero root. \n\nSo, if I can factor P(z) as z^{2k} * product_{i=1}^m (z^2 - a_i^2). Then, if I take Q(z) to be z^k * product_{i=1}^m (z - a_i), then Q(-z) would be (-z)^k * product_{i=1}^m (-z - a_i) = (-1)^k z^k * product_{i=1}^m (-1)(z + a_i) = (-1)^{k + m} z^k * product_{i=1}^m (z + a_i).\n\nThen, multiplying Q(z) and Q(-z):\n\nQ(z)Q(-z) = [z^k product_{i=1}^m (z - a_i)] * [(-1)^{k + m} z^k product_{i=1}^m (z + a_i)] = (-1)^{k + m} z^{2k} product_{i=1}^m (z^2 - a_i^2).\n\nBut P(z) is z^{2k} product_{i=1}^m (z^2 - a_i^2). So unless (-1)^{k + m} = 1, there is a sign discrepancy. Therefore, for Q(z)Q(-z) to equal P(z), we need (-1)^{k + m} = 1. So, either k + m is even. Hmm, this suggests that depending on the number of factors and the multiplicity at zero, we might have a sign issue. \n\nWait, but P(z) is given as a polynomial with complex coefficients. In the complex plane, can we adjust Q(z) by a unit complex number to absorb the sign? Let's see. Suppose we let Q(z) = c * z^k * product_{i=1}^m (z - a_i), where c is a constant. Then Q(-z) = c * (-z)^k * product_{i=1}^m (-z - a_i) = c * (-1)^k z^k * product_{i=1}^m (-1)(z + a_i) = c * (-1)^{k + m} z^k * product_{i=1}^m (z + a_i). Then Q(z)Q(-z) = c^2 * (-1)^{k + m} z^{2k} product_{i=1}^m (z^2 - a_i^2). \n\nSince we need Q(z)Q(-z) = P(z) = z^{2k} product_{i=1}^m (z^2 - a_i^2), so we require that c^2 * (-1)^{k + m} = 1. Since we are working over the complex numbers, we can choose c such that c^2 = (-1)^{k + m}. Therefore, c can be chosen as either sqrt((-1)^{k + m}) or any square root. In complex numbers, square roots always exist, so we can pick such a c. Therefore, Q(z) can be constructed by including this constant c. \n\nTherefore, the construction works. So starting from an even polynomial P(z), we can factor it as Q(z)Q(-z) where Q(z) is constructed by taking half the roots (one from each pair {a, -a}) and including a suitable constant factor to adjust the sign. \n\nBut wait, is that all? Let me check with an example. Suppose P(z) = z^2. Then P(z) is even. Let's try to write it as Q(z)Q(-z). Let Q(z) = az + b. Then Q(-z) = -az + b. Multiplying them: (-az + b)(az + b) = -a^2 z^2 + b^2. To get z^2, we need -a^2 z^2 + b^2 = z^2. So coefficients: -a^2 = 1, and b^2 = 0. But this would require a^2 = -1, which is possible in complex numbers (a = i or a = -i), and b = 0. Therefore, Q(z) = i z or Q(z) = -i z. Then Q(z)Q(-z) = (i z)(-i (-z)) = (i z)(i z) = i^2 z^2 = -z^2. Wait, that's -z^2. Hmm, but we need z^2. So maybe Q(z) = z. Then Q(-z) = -z, and Q(z)Q(-z) = -z^2. Still not z^2. Hmm, so maybe my previous reasoning missed something here.\n\nWait, P(z) = z^2. If we take Q(z) = i z, then Q(z)Q(-z) = (i z)(-i z) = -i^2 z^2 = -(-1) z^2 = z^2. There we go. So Q(z) = i z works. Therefore, Q(z) is a polynomial with complex coefficients, which is allowed here. So even though the coefficients of Q(z) might be complex, the problem doesn't restrict Q(z) to real coefficients. Therefore, even if P(z) has real coefficients, Q(z) might need to have complex coefficients. \n\nWait, but the problem statement just says \"there exists a polynomial Q(z)\", without specifying real or complex coefficients. So even if P(z) has real coefficients, Q(z) might need to be complex. For example, P(z) = z^2 + 1. Then Q(z) could be z + i, then Q(-z) = -z + i, and their product is (-z + i)(z + i) = -z^2 - iz + iz + i^2 = -z^2 -1, which is -P(z). So that's not right. Hmm, but perhaps if we take Q(z) = (1 + i)/\u221a2 z + something? Wait, maybe another approach. Let's factor P(z) = z^2 + 1 = (z + i)(z - i). But these are factors as (z - i)(z + i) = z^2 +1. But how to write this as Q(z)Q(-z). Let's try Q(z) = z + i. Then Q(-z) = -z + i. Then Q(z)Q(-z) = (-z + i)(z + i) = -z^2 - iz + iz + i^2 = -z^2 -1, which is -P(z). Not quite. So perhaps scaling Q(z) by a constant factor. Let Q(z) = a(z + i). Then Q(-z) = a(-z + i). Then Q(z)Q(-z) = a^2 (-z^2 -1). So if we set a^2 = -1, then Q(z)Q(-z) = z^2 +1. So a = i or -i. So Q(z) = i(z + i) = iz -1. Then Q(-z) = -iz -1. Multiply them: (iz -1)(-iz -1) = -i^2 z^2 - iz + iz +1 = z^2 +1. Perfect. So Q(z) = iz -1. So even though P(z) has real coefficients, Q(z) can have complex coefficients. Therefore, the existence is possible in complex polynomials.\n\nSo in the previous case, the key was to choose the constant factor appropriately. So even if the polynomial P(z) has real coefficients, Q(z) might need complex coefficients. But the problem doesn't restrict Q(z) to real coefficients, so that's okay. Therefore, in general, as per the earlier reasoning, if P is even, then it can be written as Q(z)Q(-z) by grouping the roots into pairs {a, -a}, taking one root from each pair to form Q(z), including the even-powered roots at zero, and adjusting the constant factor to account for any sign issues. Since we can take square roots in the complex plane, the constant can always be adjusted so that Q(z)Q(-z) equals P(z).\n\nAnother example: Take P(z) = (z^2 -1)(z^2 -4) = z^4 -5z^2 +4. Then Q(z) could be (z -1)(z -2). Then Q(-z) = (-z -1)(-z -2) = (z +1)(z +2). Then Q(z)Q(-z) = (z -1)(z -2)(z +1)(z +2) = (z^2 -1)(z^2 -4) = P(z). So in this case, Q(z) is (z -1)(z -2), and it works. But here, Q(z) has real coefficients because the roots are symmetric in real numbers. However, if the roots are complex, then Q(z) would have complex coefficients. For example, P(z) = (z^2 +1)(z^2 +4). Then Q(z) = (z - i)(z - 2i), Q(-z) = (-z -i)(-z -2i) = (z +i)(z +2i). Then Q(z)Q(-z) = (z^2 +1)(z^2 +4) = P(z). Here, Q(z) has complex coefficients. So it works.\n\nSo, in general, the approach is:\n\n1. Since P(z) is even, all its roots come in pairs {a, -a}, and any root at 0 has even multiplicity.\n2. Therefore, P(z) can be factored as z^{2k} * product_{i=1}^m (z^2 - a_i^2).\n3. Then, define Q(z) as z^k * product_{i=1}^m (z - a_i), multiplied by a suitable constant c.\n4. Then Q(-z) is (-z)^k * product_{i=1}^m (-z - a_i) = (-1)^k z^k * (-1)^m product_{i=1}^m (z + a_i) = (-1)^{k + m} z^k product_{i=1}^m (z + a_i).\n5. Thus, Q(z)Q(-z) = c^2 (-1)^{k + m} z^{2k} product_{i=1}^m (z^2 - a_i^2).\n6. Since P(z) = z^{2k} product_{i=1}^m (z^2 - a_i^2), set c^2 (-1)^{k + m} = 1. Since we are in complex numbers, we can choose c = [ (-1)^{k + m} ]^{-1/2}, which exists because any complex number has a square root.\n\nTherefore, Q(z) exists, which is the polynomial c z^k product_{i=1}^m (z - a_i). Hence, the converse holds.\n\nTherefore, combining both directions, we have proven that a polynomial P(z) is even if and only if there exists a polynomial Q(z) such that P(z) = Q(z)Q(-z).\n\nBut let me check if there are any edge cases. For example, if P(z) is a constant polynomial. Suppose P(z) = c. Since it's even, then for it to be written as Q(z)Q(-z), Q(z) must be a constant polynomial as well. Let Q(z) = d. Then Q(-z) = d, so Q(z)Q(-z) = d^2. Therefore, c must be a square in the complex numbers. But in complex numbers, every constant is a square. So we can always choose d such that d^2 = c. Therefore, even constant polynomials can be expressed this way. So that's covered.\n\nAnother case: P(z) = 0. Then Q(z) can be the zero polynomial, since 0 * 0 = 0. So that works too.\n\nTherefore, all cases are covered, and the proof holds.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that a polynomial \\( P(z) \\) is even if and only if there exists a polynomial \\( Q(z) \\) such that \\( P(z) \\equiv Q(z) \\cdot Q(-z) \\), we proceed as follows:\n\n**Forward Direction (\\( \\Rightarrow \\)):**  \nAssume \\( P(z) \\) is even, i.e., \\( P(z) = P(-z) \\) for all \\( z \\in \\mathbb{C} \\). Since \\( P(z) \\) is even, all its terms have even degrees, and its roots are symmetric about the origin (if \\( a \\) is a root, so is \\( -a \\)). The zero root (if present) has even multiplicity. Thus, \\( P(z) \\) can be factored as:\n\\[\nP(z) = c \\cdot z^{2k} \\prod_{i=1}^m (z^2 - a_i^2),\n\\]\nwhere \\( c \\in \\mathbb{C} \\), \\( k \\in \\mathbb{N} \\), and \\( a_i \\neq 0 \\). Define:\n\\[\nQ(z) = d \\cdot z^k \\prod_{i=1}^m (z - a_i),\n\\]\nwhere \\( d \\) is chosen such that \\( d^2 = c \\cdot (-1)^{k + m} \\). Then:\n\\[\nQ(-z) = d \\cdot (-z)^k \\prod_{i=1}^m (-z - a_i) = d \\cdot (-1)^k z^k \\cdot (-1)^m \\prod_{i=1}^m (z + a_i) = d \\cdot (-1)^{k + m} z^k \\prod_{i=1}^m (z + a_i).\n\\]\nMultiplying \\( Q(z) \\) and \\( Q(-z) \\):\n\\[\nQ(z)Q(-z) = d^2 \\cdot (-1)^{k + m} z^{2k} \\prod_{i=1}^m (z^2 - a_i^2) = c \\cdot z^{2k} \\prod_{i=1}^m (z^2 - a_i^2) = P(z).\n\\]\nThus, \\( Q(z) \\) exists such that \\( P(z) = Q(z)Q(-z) \\).\n\n**Reverse Direction (\\( \\Leftarrow \\)):**  \nSuppose \\( P(z) = Q(z)Q(-z) \\) for some polynomial \\( Q(z) \\). Then:\n\\[\nP(-z) = Q(-z)Q(-(-z)) = Q(-z)Q(z) = Q(z)Q(-z) = P(z),\n\\]\nproving \\( P(z) \\) is even.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nA polynomial \\( P(z) \\) is even if and only if there exists a polynomial \\( Q(z) \\) such that \\( P(z) \\equiv Q(z) \\cdot Q(-z) \\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{P(z) \\text{ is even } \\iff \\exists Q(z) \\text{ such that } P(z) = Q(z)Q(-z) \\text{ for all } z \\in \\mathbb{C}.}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet's prove the given statement in a structured manner:\n\n1. **Sufficiency Part**:\n\n   We need to show that if $P(z) \\equiv Q(z) \\cdot Q(-z)$, then $P(z)$ is an even function.\n\n   - Suppose $P(z) \\equiv Q(z) \\cdot Q(-z)$. We have:\n     \\[\n     P(-z) = Q(-z) \\cdot Q(z)\n     \\]\n     By the commutative property of multiplication, we can rearrange the terms as:\n     \\[\n     P(-z) = Q(z) \\cdot Q(-z) \\equiv P(z)\n     \\]\n     This confirms that $P(z)$ is indeed an even function.\n\n2. **Necessity Part**:\n\n   Conversely, we need to show that if $P(z)$ is an even function, there exists a polynomial $Q(z)$ such that $P(z) \\equiv Q(z) \\cdot Q(-z)$.\n\n   - First, consider the case when $P(z) \\equiv 0$. Here we can trivially take $Q(z) \\equiv 0$.\n   - Assume now that $P(z)$ is a non-zero even polynomial and let $m$ be the number of distinct non-zero roots of $P(z)$. We will use mathematical induction on $m$ to prove the existence of $Q(z)$.\n\n3. **Base Case** ($m = 0$):\n\n   - When $m = 0$, $P(z)$ has the form $P(z) = a z^n$ where $a \\neq 0$ and $n$ is even (since $P(z)$ is even). So, $P(z) = a z^{2k}$ for some $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}^+$. \n   - Choose $Q(z) = b z^k$ where $b^2 = (-1)^k a$. Then:\n     \\[\n     Q(z) \\cdot Q(-z) = b z^k \\cdot b (-z)^k = b^2 z^{2k} = (-1)^k a z^{2k} = a z^{2k} = P(z)\n     \\]\n     Thus, $Q(z)$ satisfies the given condition.\n\n4. **Induction Step**:\n\n   - Assume the statement holds for all even polynomials with fewer than $m$ distinct non-zero roots. Now consider $P(z)$ with $m$ distinct non-zero roots.\n   - Given that $\\alpha$ is a non-zero root of $P(z)$, we know $P(z) = (z - \\alpha)(z + \\alpha) R(z)$ for some polynomial $R(z)$, since $P(z)$ is even:\n     \\[\n     P(-z) = (z + \\alpha)(z - \\alpha) R(-z) = P(z) \\implies R(-z) \\equiv R(z)\n     \\]\n\n   - By the induction hypothesis, since $R(z)$ has fewer than $m$ roots, there exists $S(z)$ such that $R(z) \\equiv S(z) \\cdot S(-z)$.\n   - Define $Q(z) = \\mathrm{i}(z - \\alpha) S(z)$. Then:\n     \\[\n     Q(z) \\cdot Q(-z) = \\mathrm{i}(z - \\alpha) S(z) \\cdot \\mathrm{i}(-z - \\alpha) S(-z) = (z - \\alpha)(z + \\alpha) S(z) S(-z) = P(z)\n     \\]\n\nConclusively, the necessity part is proved, and thus completing the proof of the problem statement.\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Q.E.D.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A point \\( M \\) is marked on a plane, not lying on the coordinate axes. Point \\( Q \\) moves along the y-axis, and point \\( P \\) moves along the x-axis such that the angle \\( \\angle PMQ \\) always remains a right angle. Find the locus of points \\( N \\), which are symmetric to \\( M \\) with respect to the line \\( PQ \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this geometry problem here. Let me try to understand it step by step. The problem says:\n\n\"A point M is marked on a plane, not lying on the coordinate axes. Point Q moves along the y-axis, and point P moves along the x-axis such that the angle \u2220PMQ always remains a right angle. Find the locus of points N, which are symmetric to M with respect to the line PQ.\"\n\nFirst, let me parse what's being asked. We have a fixed point M somewhere in the plane, not on the x or y-axis. Then there are two moving points: Q on the y-axis and P on the x-axis. These points P and Q move in such a way that the angle at M, \u2220PMQ, is always a right angle. For each such position of P and Q, we need to find the reflection of M over the line PQ, which is point N, and then determine the path that all such points N trace out\u2014the locus.\n\nAlright, so the key steps here seem to be:\n\n1. Express the coordinates of P and Q such that \u2220PMQ is 90 degrees.\n2. For each valid P and Q, find the reflection N of M over line PQ.\n3. Determine the equation that describes all possible N points, eliminating the parameters related to P and Q.\n\nLet me start by setting up a coordinate system. Let\u2019s assume M has coordinates (a, b), where a \u2260 0 and b \u2260 0 since it's not on the axes. Let\u2019s denote point P as (p, 0) on the x-axis and point Q as (0, q) on the y-axis.\n\nGiven that angle PMQ is a right angle. So, the vectors MP and MQ should be perpendicular. Let me confirm that. The angle at M between PM and QM is 90 degrees. Therefore, vectors MP and MQ are perpendicular.\n\nWait, vectors MP and MQ. Vector MP goes from M to P, which is (p - a, 0 - b). Vector MQ goes from M to Q, which is (0 - a, q - b). The dot product of these vectors should be zero.\n\nSo, (p - a)(-a) + (-b)(q - b) = 0.\n\nCalculating that:\n\n-(p - a)a - b(q - b) = 0\n\nExpanding:\n\n-pa + a\u00b2 - bq + b\u00b2 = 0\n\nRearranged:\n\npa + bq = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2\n\nSo this is the condition that relates p and q. For any Q moving along the y-axis, there is a corresponding P on the x-axis such that this equation holds, and vice versa.\n\nTherefore, for each q, p can be determined as p = (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 - bq)/a, provided a \u2260 0. Similarly, q can be expressed in terms of p.\n\nSo, that's the relation between p and q. Now, the next part is to find the reflection of M over line PQ. The reflection of a point over a line. Let me recall the formula for reflection over a line.\n\nGiven a line in the form Ax + By + C = 0, the reflection of a point (x0, y0) is given by:\n\n(x', y') = (x0 - 2A(Ax0 + By0 + C)/(A\u00b2 + B\u00b2), y0 - 2B(Ax0 + By0 + C)/(A\u00b2 + B\u00b2))\n\nAlternatively, if the line is given in another form, like two-point form, perhaps it's easier to compute using vectors or coordinate geometry.\n\nSince PQ is a line connecting points P(p, 0) and Q(0, q), its equation can be written as x/p + y/q = 1, provided p and q are not zero. But since P is on x-axis and Q on y-axis, p and q can't be zero because then PQ would coincide with the axes, but M is not on the axes, so angle PMQ wouldn't make sense if P or Q were at the origin. Wait, actually, can p or q be zero? If p is zero, then P would coincide with the origin, but the problem states that Q moves along the y-axis and P along the x-axis; they can be at the origin. However, if P is at the origin, then line PQ would be along the y-axis from origin to Q, which is the same as the y-axis. Similarly, if Q is at the origin, PQ would be along the x-axis. But M is not on the axes, so reflecting over the x or y-axis would just flip the coordinates. But since the problem says that N is the reflection of M over PQ, which could be the axes if P or Q is at the origin. But perhaps in such cases, the reflection is straightforward. However, given the condition \u2220PMQ is right angle, let's see if p or q can be zero.\n\nSuppose p = 0. Then point P is at (0, 0). Then angle PMQ is the angle at M between PM and QM. PM would be the vector from M to P, which is (-a, -b), and QM is the vector from M to Q, which is (0 - a, q - b). The dot product would be (-a)(-a) + (-b)(q - b) = a\u00b2 - b(q - b) = a\u00b2 - bq + b\u00b2. For this to be zero (since angle is 90 degrees), a\u00b2 - bq + b\u00b2 = 0 => bq = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 => q = (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/b. So, if p=0, then Q must be at (0, (a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/b). Similarly, if q=0, then point Q is at origin, and P must be at ((a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)/a, 0). Therefore, in these cases, the line PQ would be the x-axis or y-axis, but only at specific positions. So, in general, p and q can be zero, but only when the other point is adjusted accordingly.\n\nBut in the problem statement, Q moves along the y-axis and P moves along the x-axis such that angle PMQ remains right. So, in their movement, p and q are related by pa + bq = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2. So, they can take any values as long as this equation is satisfied, including p=0 or q=0 as above.\n\nBut the locus of N is supposed to cover all possible reflections as P and Q move under this condition. So, even if in some cases PQ coincides with an axis, those reflections are included. So, perhaps the locus includes those points as well.\n\nAnyway, moving on. To find the reflection N of M over line PQ. Let me try to derive the coordinates of N given line PQ.\n\nFirst, line PQ passes through points P(p, 0) and Q(0, q). Its equation can be written as:\n\ny = (-q/p)x + q\n\nOr, rearranged: qx + py = pq\n\nSo, the standard form is qx + py - pq = 0\n\nTherefore, coefficients are A = q, B = p, C = -pq\n\nSo, the reflection formula would be:\n\nx' = a - 2q(qa + pb - pq)/(q\u00b2 + p\u00b2)\n\ny' = b - 2p(qa + pb - pq)/(q\u00b2 + p\u00b2)\n\nWait, let me check. The general formula is:\n\nx' = x0 - 2A(Ax0 + By0 + C)/(A\u00b2 + B\u00b2)\n\ny' = y0 - 2B(Ax0 + By0 +