e formula for even n=2p, the coefficient for y^{2p -2} should be -C_{2p -1}^{1} = - (2p -1), which matches.\n\nThird term coefficient for y^{2p -4}: C_{2p -3}^{2} + C_{2p -3}^{1} = \\binom{2p -3}{2} + \\binom{2p -3}{1}.\n\nUsing the identity \\( \\binom{n}{k} + \\binom{n}{k -1} = \\binom{n +1}{k} \\).\n\nHere, n=2p -3, k=2:\n\n\\binom{2p -3}{2} + \\binom{2p -3}{1} = \\binom{2p -2}{2}.\n\nBut according to the formula, the coefficient should be C_{2p -2}^{2} = \\binom{2p -2}{2}, which matches.\n\nSimilarly, proceeding recursively, each coefficient combines using the binomial coefficient identity, leading to the correct terms.\n\nThe last term:\n\nIn y f_m(x), the last term is (-1)^{p -1} C_{p}^{p -1} y^{2}.\n\nIn f_{m-1}(x), the last term is (-1)^{p -1} (since n=2p -2, so (n)/2 =p -1).\n\nTherefore, subtracting f_{m-1}(x) adds a term + (-1)^{p} (due to the negative sign outside).\n\nBut need to verify the last term:\n\nLet's track the last term of y f_m(x): which is (-1)^{p -1} C_{p}^{p -1} y^{2}.\n\nSubtracting the last term of f_{m-1}(x): which is (-1)^{p -1}.\n\nThus, the combined term is (-1)^{p -1} C_{p}^{p -1} y^{2} - (-1)^{p -1} = (-1)^{p -1} [ C_{p}^{p -1} y^{2} -1 ].\n\nBut according to the formula for f_{m+1}(x)=f_{2p}(x), the last term is (-1)^p.\n\nWait, this part seems tricky. Let's take an example with p=2, so m+1=4, m=3, m-1=2.\n\nCompute f_4(x)=y f_3(x) -f_2(x).\n\nf_3(x)=y^3 -2y.\n\ny f_3(x)=y^4 -2y^2.\n\nSubtract f_2(x)=y^2 -1:\n\ny^4 -2y^2 - y^2 +1 = y^4 -3y^2 +1.\n\nAccording to the formula for n=4 (even):\n\ny^4 - C_{3}^1 y^2 + C_{2}^2 y^0 = y^4 -3y^2 +1, which matches.\n\nIn this case, the last term comes from the constant term in f_{m-1}(x) being -1, and when subtracted, it becomes +1.\n\nIn the general case, the last term of f_{m-1}(x) (even) is (-1)^{(m-1)/2} when m-1 is even. Wait, when m+1=2p, m-1=2p-2. For even n=2p-2, the last term is (-1)^{(2p-2)/2}=(-1)^{p-1}.\n\nTherefore, in the expression y f_m(x) - f_{m-1}(x), the last term from f_{m