e \u2264 N. So N is at least 31.\n\nBut person 7 and person 14 are also there, so N must be at least 31. So the total number of people is some N \u226531, and we have to find N such that the distance from 31 to 7 is the same as the distance from 31 to 14.\n\nSo the distance between two people is the minimal number of steps between them, either clockwise or counter-clockwise. But since the seating is circular, the distance can be calculated as the minimum of (|a - b|, N - |a - b|). Wait, but actually, since the numbers are arranged in a circle, the distance between person a and person b is the minimum of ( (b - a) mod N, (a - b) mod N ). But maybe in terms of steps.\n\nBut maybe in this problem, the distance is measured as the number of people between them when moving clockwise, but considering the circle. Wait, but the problem says \"the distance from him to the person with the card numbered 7 is the same as the distance to the person with the card numbered 14\". So, maybe the number of seats between 31 and 7 clockwise is the same as the number of seats between 31 and 14 clockwise? Or maybe the minimal distance, regardless of direction.\n\nBut to clarify, in a circular arrangement, the distance can be measured in two directions. So if the minimal distance in either direction is the same for both pairs (31 to 7 and 31 to 14), then we have a condition to find N.\n\nLet's formalize this.\n\nLet\u2019s denote the positions as numbers from 1 to N arranged clockwise around the table. The distance between two positions i and j is the minimal of |i - j| and N - |i - j|. But since the numbers are arranged in a circle, moving clockwise, the distance from i to j could be (j - i) mod N if we go clockwise, or (i - j) mod N if we go counter-clockwise. The minimal of these two is the distance.\n\nBut in the problem, person 31 is equidistant to 7 and 14. So, the minimal distance from 31 to 7 is equal to the minimal distance from 31 to 14.\n\nSo, let's compute the two distances.\n\n