ines forming a 30-degree angle, K is located such that if I drop perpendiculars from K to each side, their lengths are 1 and 2. \n\nNow, through point K, we can draw various lines that intersect both sides of the angle. Each such line will form a triangle with the vertex A. The task is to find the line through K that creates the triangle with the minimum area. Got it. So, among all possible lines passing through K intersecting both sides of the angle, which one gives the smallest possible area of the triangle formed with A?\n\nLet me start by setting up a coordinate system to model this situation. Maybe placing vertex A at the origin. Let's consider one side of the angle along the positive x-axis and the other side making a 30-degree angle with the x-axis. So, the two sides of the angle can be represented by the x-axis and a line making 30 degrees with the x-axis.\n\nPoint K is inside this angle, and its distances to both sides are 1 and 2. Wait, but which distance corresponds to which side? The problem says \"distances to the sides of the angle are 1 and 2\". Since the angle has two sides, K is at distances 1 and 2 from each of them. But does it matter which distance is to which side? Maybe not, but perhaps the coordinates of K can be determined based on these distances.\n\nTo find the coordinates of K, I can use the formula for the distance from a point to a line. Let's assume that the two sides of the angle are the x-axis and a line making a 30-degree angle with the x-axis. Let me first write the equations of these two sides.\n\nThe first side is the x-axis: y = 0.\n\nThe second side makes a 30-degree angle with the x-axis, so its slope is tan(30\u00b0) = 1/\u221a3. Therefore, the equation of the second side is y = (1/\u221a3)x.\n\nNow, point K is somewhere inside the angle. Let's denote the coordinates of K as (h, k). The distance from K to the x-axis (y=0) is simply |k|. Similarly, the distance from K to the other side (y = (1/\u221a3)x) can be calculated using