ically zero, which it's not, then the solution sets would be different. Therefore, Q with negative proportionality does not lead to P. Therefore, to have Q imply P, the proportionality constant must be positive. However, Q as stated only requires that the ratios are equal, not that they are positive. Therefore, Q is not a sufficient condition unless we know the constant is positive.\n\nBut wait, in the problem statement, Q is stated as a\u2081/a\u2082 = b\u2081/b\u2082 = c\u2081/c\u2082. If we interpret this ratio as being non-zero, but not necessarily positive, then Q would include both positive and negative proportionality. Therefore, Q is not sufficient because if the ratio is negative, the inequalities would have opposite solution sets. Therefore, Q is not sufficient. Wait, but maybe I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, actually, if the coefficients are proportional with a negative constant, then the quadratic is a scalar multiple with a negative scalar. So, multiplying the quadratic by a negative scalar would flip the inequality. Therefore, the solution set of a\u2081x\u00b2 + b\u2081x + c\u2081 >0 would be the same as the solution set of -a\u2082x\u00b2 -b\u2082x -c\u2082 >0, which is equivalent to a\u2082x\u00b2 + b\u2082x + c\u2082 <0. Therefore, unless the solution sets of a\u2082x\u00b2 + b\u2082x + c\u2082 >0 and <0 are the same, which they aren't (unless the quadratic is zero everywhere, which it's not), the solution sets would differ. Therefore, Q with a negative ratio would not result in P. Therefore, Q is not sufficient for P unless the ratio is positive. Therefore, the answer may be that Q is not sufficient. But since Q allows the ratio to be negative, which would not satisfy P, Q is not a sufficient condition. However, if the ratio is positive, then Q would imply P. So, is Q in general a sufficient condition? No, because depending on the sign of the ratio, it may or may not be. Therefore, Q is not a sufficient condition unless we have the ratio positive.