hat results in the cubic equation having exactly one real root. Therefore, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{No}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "### Problem:\n\nSergeev I.N.\n\nOn the board, the following polynomial is written: $x^3 + \\ldots x^2 + \\ldots x + \\ldots = 0$. Two students take turns filling in the ellipses with real numbers. The goal of the first student is to get an equation that has exactly one real root. Can the second student prevent him from doing so?\n\n1. The first student can ensure that the equation has exactly one root, regardless of the actions of the second student, by setting the coefficient of $x^2$ to 0 on his first turn.\n   \n2. After this initial move, the equation reduces to the form $x^3 + bx + c = 0$.\n\n3. The second player will then choose either the constant term $c$ or the coefficient $b$ of $x$. \n\n4. **Case 1:** If the second player sets the constant term $c$, the first student should set the coefficient of $x$ to 0 on his next move, resulting in the polynomial:\n   \\[\n   x^3 + c = 0\n   \\]\n   This equation, $y = x^3 + c$, increases monotonically over the entire real axis. Thus, it has exactly one real root, regardless of the value of $c$ chosen.\n\n5. **Case 2:** If the second player sets the coefficient $b$ of $x$ instead, the first student must choose an appropriate constant term $c$ to ensure the polynomial:\n   \\[\n   x^3 + bx + c = 0\n   \\]\n   has exactly one real root.\n\n6. If $b \\geq 0$, the function $f(x) = x^3 + bx$ is monotonically increasing because its derivative:\n   \\[\n   f'(x) = 3x^2 + b \\geq 0\n   \\]\n   is always non-negative. Therefore, the equation $f(x) = 0$ has exactly one real root for any value of $c$.\n\n7. If $b < 0$, the function $f(x) = x^3 + bx + c$ has a local minimum. Let\u2019s denote the minimum value by $-m$, occurring at some point $x_0$. The derivative:\n   \\[\n   f'(x) = 3x^2 + b\n   \\]\n   has a unique minimum, making $f(x)$ decrease until $x = x_0$ and increase thereafter.