fy the denominator:**\n\n   To simplify \\(1 + \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\):\n   \\[\n   1 + \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = 1 + \\frac{2 \\cdot 1}{5 \\cdot 2} = 1 + \\frac{2}{10} = 1 + \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{5}{5} + \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{6}{5}.\n   \\]\n\n7. **Combine the results to find \\(\\tan (\\beta - 2\\alpha)\\):**\n\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   \\tan (\\beta - 2\\alpha) = \\frac{\\frac{-1}{10}}{\\frac{6}{5}} = \\frac{-1}{10} \\cdot \\frac{5}{6} = \\frac{-1 \\cdot 5}{10 \\cdot 6} = \\frac{-5}{60} = -\\frac{1}{12}.\n   \\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{1}{12}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Which of the numbers is greater: \\(31^{11}\\) or \\(17^{14}\\)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, so I need to figure out whether 31 to the power of 11 is greater than 17 to the power of 14, or the other way around. Hmm, these are both pretty big numbers, so calculating them directly isn't practical. I should think of another way to compare them without actually computing the entire values. \n\nLet me start by recalling some mathematical strategies for comparing exponents. One common method is to take logarithms of both numbers because logarithms preserve the order of numbers (since logarithmic functions are monotonically increasing). If I take the natural log or logarithm base 10 of both, I can compare the results to see which is larger. That might work. Let me write that down.\n\nSo, if I take the natural logarithm of both numbers:\n\nln(31^11) = 11 * ln(31)\nln(17^14) = 14 * ln(17)\n\nThen, I can compute these two products and see which is larger. Since ln(31) and ln(17) are constants, I can approximate them using known values or a calculator. Wait, but maybe I can do this without a calculator? Let me think if I can find approximate values for these logarithms.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a way to express both numbers with exponents that can be compared more easily. For example