ed under translation by some vectors? But since it's finite, that might not work unless it's a lattice, but lattices are infinite.\n\nWait, but the problem says \"finite set\". So lattice points are out. So how to have a finite set where every direction vector is repeated. Maybe considering points arranged in such a way that every vector between two points has another pair with the same vector.\n\nThis is similar to the concept of a \"parallelogram system\" or something, but in 3D.\n\nAlternatively, think of the example in 2D: a regular polygon. But in 2D, for example, a square: each edge has a parallel edge, each face diagonal has a parallel one. But the space diagonals are unique. Wait, in a square, the diagonals are only two, but they are perpendicular. So in 2D, a square doesn't satisfy the condition because the diagonals are not parallel to any other line. So even in 2D, the square wouldn't work. However, if we take a rectangle, edges are parallel, but diagonals are also two, which are parallel? Wait, in a rectangle, the two diagonals are congruent but not parallel unless it's a square. Wait, no, in a rectangle, the diagonals are congruent but not parallel. They intersect at the center. So in 2D, perhaps a hexagon? In a regular hexagon, each edge is parallel to the one opposite, and each diagonal is parallel to another. Wait, let's see. In a regular hexagon, the long diagonals (spanning three edges) are all parallel in pairs? Wait, no. In a regular hexagon, the diagonals can be of different lengths. The ones that go across two edges are parallel to each other, and the ones that go across three edges (the diameter) are all the same line, actually. Wait, no, in a regular hexagon, the longest diagonals are those that pass through the center and connect opposite vertices, so there are three such diagonals, each pair intersecting at 60 degrees. So they are not parallel. So even in a regular hexagon, the longest diagonals are not parallel. So perhaps in 2D, there is no finite set of points where every line segment has another parallel to it. Except maybe a set of two points, but trivial.\n\nBut the problem is in 3D, and the set must not be coplanar. So maybe 3D allows for more configurations.\n\nWait, consider a set of points forming two separate regular figures in parallel planes. For example, two squares in parallel planes, aligned such that corresponding vertices are connected by vertical edges. Then, similar to a cube, but open. Wait, but in that case, similar to a cube, the vertical edges are parallel, the horizontal edges in each square are parallel to their counterparts in the other square, and the face diagonals would also be parallel. However, the space diagonals between the two squares might not have parallels. Wait, for example, taking a point in the top square and a non-corresponding point in the bottom square. The vector might be unique.\n\nAlternatively, if the two squares are offset, like in a cube, but just two layers. Hmm. Wait, but if we have two squares in parallel planes, each with four points, and connected by vertical edges, that's a cube without the top and bottom faces. But then the space diagonals would still be present when connecting non-vertical points. For example, connecting a top square vertex to a bottom square non-corresponding vertex. Those vectors might not have parallels.\n\nAlternatively, think of a octahedron. A regular octahedron has six vertices, twelve edges. Each edge is part of a triangle. In a regular octahedron, the edges are all equivalent, but in terms of direction vectors, they come in pairs. For example, in a regular octahedron with vertices at (\u00b11,0,0), (0,\u00b11,0), (0,0,\u00b11), the edges are between each pair of adjacent vertices. The direction vectors would be like (1,1,0), (-1,1,0), etc. Wait, actually, in a regular octahedron, the edges are between points that differ in two coordinates by 1 and 0. Wait, maybe it's better to think of a octahedron as two pyramids glued together. So, the edges are from the top vertex to the base vertices, and the base edges. In this case, the base edges are all in one plane and form a square. Wait, but in a regular octahedron, all edges are the same length. The direction vectors from the top vertex to the base vertices would be, say, (1,1,1), (-1,1,1), etc., but normalized. Hmm, this might not help. Maybe the regular octahedron still has unique direction vectors for some edges.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps considering a set of points forming the vertices of a rectangular prism, but with more points. Wait, if we take a prism over a regular polygon, but in 3D. For example, a hexagonal prism. But again, adding more points might introduce more directions, but unless each direction is repeated, it won't satisfy the condition. The problem is, every time you add a point, you potentially add many new direction vectors. So maybe it's difficult to have a finite set where every direction is repeated.\n\nAlternatively, think of a set of points that form a finite vector space. For example, a set closed under vector addition. But finite vector spaces are like finite fields, but arranging points in space to form such a structure might be complex. Wait, but in three-dimensional space over real numbers, you can't have a finite vector space. Unless considering points over a finite field, but the problem doesn't specify that.\n\nAlternatively, consider the example of the vertices of a regular octahedron. Let's analyze it. A regular octahedron has six vertices: two poles (0,0,\u00b11) and four equatorial points (\u00b11,0,0), (0,\u00b11,0). Let's compute the direction vectors between all pairs.\n\nFirst, the edges from the poles to the equatorial points: e.g., (0,0,1) to (1,0,0) has direction vector (1,0,-1). Similarly, (0,0,1) to (0,1,0) is (0,1,-1), etc. Each of these edges has four different direction vectors. Then, the edges between equatorial points: (1,0,0) to (0,1,0) is (-1,1,0), and similarly others. Each of these edges has direction vectors like (\u00b11, \u00b11, 0). Then, the diagonals: for example, (1,0,0) to (-1,0,0) is (-2,0,0), but that's just a line through the origin, but in terms of direction vector, it's (-2,0,0), which is the same as (-1,0,0). Similarly, other diameters. Wait, but in the octahedron, the edges between the equatorial points are distance sqrt(2) apart, and the ones from the poles are sqrt(2) as well. Wait, in a regular octahedron, all edges are of equal length, so maybe direction vectors are scaled versions. Wait, but direction vectors can be different even if lengths are the same.\n\nFor example, from (0,0,1) to (1,0,0) is vector (1,0,-1), length sqrt(2). From (1,0,0) to (0,1,0) is vector (-1,1,0), length sqrt(2). So these have different direction vectors. Therefore, in the octahedron, the edges have different direction vectors. So, for each edge, there isn't necessarily another edge with the same direction. For example, the edge from (0,0,1) to (1,0,0) has direction vector (1,0,-1). Is there another edge with the same direction vector? The edge from (0,0,-1) to (-1,0,0) would be direction vector (-1,0,1), which is the negative. So lines in opposite directions. But are those considered parallel? Yes, because lines are parallel if their direction vectors are scalar multiples. So (1,0,-1) and (-1,0,1) are scalar multiples (scalar -1), so the lines are parallel. So in that case, yes, each edge from the north pole to an equatorial point is parallel to an edge from the south pole to the opposite equatorial point.\n\nSimilarly, edges between equatorial points: the edge from (1,0,0) to (0,1,0) is direction vector (-1,1,0), and the edge from (-1,0,0) to (0,-1,0) is (1,-1,0), which is again a scalar multiple (-1), so parallel. So in the octahedron, every edge has another edge which is antiparallel, hence parallel.\n\nBut wait, the problem states \"lines AB and CD are parallel\". Since lines are not directed, antiparallel vectors still make the lines parallel. So in the octahedron, for any edge AB, there is an edge CD which is antiparallel, hence parallel. So maybe the octahedron works? But wait, the problem says \"for each two points A, B \u2208 M\", not just edges. So we have to consider all pairs, not just edges.\n\nIn the octahedron, the vertices are two poles and four equatorial. So total of six points. The pairs of points include edges (as above) and also the diagonals. For example, the distance between two equatorial points: (1,0,0) and (0,1,0) is sqrt(2), but that's an edge. Wait, in the regular octahedron, the edges are between the poles and equatorial points, and between adjacent equatorial points. Wait, no, actually in the regular octahedron, the edges are between each pair of adjacent equatorial points. Wait, no, hold on. Let me confirm:\n\nIn a regular octahedron, there are 12 edges: each of the 6 vertices is connected to 4 others. Wait, no, actually, in a regular octahedron, each of the two poles is connected to four equatorial points, and each equatorial point is connected to two adjacent equatorial points. So total edges: 2 poles * 4 edges + 4 equatorial points * 2 edges / 2 (since each equatorial edge is counted twice) = 8 + 4 = 12 edges. So, 12 edges. But the problem requires considering all pairs, not just edges. So total pairs of points: C(6,2)=15. So, 15 lines. The edges are 12, and the remaining 3 are the diameters between opposite equatorial points: (1,0,0) to (-1,0,0), (0,1,0) to (0,-1,0), (0,0,1) to (0,0,-1). Wait, those are actually edges in the octahedron? Wait, no. In the regular octahedron, the edges are from each pole to the four equatorial points, and between adjacent equatorial points. So the diameters between opposite equatorial points are not edges. For example, (1,0,0) to (-1,0,0) is a diameter, but in the octahedron, those two points are not connected by an edge. Instead, each equatorial point is connected to its two neighbors. So in the regular octahedron, the diameters between opposite equatorial points are not edges, so those lines are not edges but are still lines between two points in M.\n\nSo, in the regular octahedron, we have 15 pairs of points. The 12 edges and 3 diameters. Now, check if for each pair, there's another pair with a parallel line.\n\nFirst, the edges from the poles to equatorial points: as previously established, each such edge has a parallel edge from the opposite pole to the opposite equatorial point. So for example, (0,0,1) to (1,0,0) is parallel to (0,0,-1) to (-1,0,0). So those are parallel. Similarly for others.\n\nThen, edges between adjacent equatorial points: for example, (1,0,0) to (0,1,0) is parallel to (-1,0,0) to (0,-1,0), as their direction vectors are (-1,1,0) and (1,-1,0), which are negatives, hence parallel.\n\nThen, the diameters: (1,0,0) to (-1,0,0) has direction vector (-2,0,0), which is just a scalar multiple of (-1,0,0). Is there another pair with the same direction? The other diameters are (0,1,0) to (0,-1,0) with direction vector (0,-2,0), and (0,0,1) to (0,0,-1) with direction vector (0,0,-2). Each of these diameters is unique in direction. So, the line connecting (1,0,0) and (-1,0,0) is only parallel to itself, but there's no other pair with the same direction. Wait, but the problem requires that for each two points A, B, there exist two other points C, D such that AB and CD are parallel. So, for the diameter AB = (1,0,0) to (-1,0,0), we need another pair CD such that CD is parallel to AB. But in the octahedron, the only line parallel to AB would be another diameter in the same direction. But there's only one such diameter: AB itself. So, there is no other pair CD. Therefore, the octahedron doesn't satisfy the condition because those three diameters don't have parallels.\n\nTherefore, the regular octahedron also fails.\n\nHmm. So perhaps another approach. Let's consider that in order for every line segment AB to have another parallel CD, the set M must be such that the direction vectors between any two points are repeated. So, for every vector v = B - A, there must exist another pair (C, D) where D - C = v or -v. Since direction matters for the vector, but since lines are undirected, CD is the same as DC, so direction vector could be v or -v. So, essentially, for every vector between two points in M, its inverse must also exist as a vector between two other points.\n\nThis is similar to the concept of a \"difference set\" or \"symmetric difference set\" in combinatorics. If the set of vectors is closed under negation, then for every vector v, -v is also present.\n\nBut how to construct such a set in 3D with a finite number of points, not all coplanar.\n\nWait, consider the simplest case: a set of four points forming a tetrahedron. But as before, in a regular tetrahedron, each edge has a unique direction vector. So, no luck.\n\nAlternatively, consider a set of points such that for every point, there is another point diametrically opposite with respect to some center. Like a centrally symmetric set. For example, the cube is centrally symmetric: for every point (x,y,z), there is a point (-x,-y,-z). In such a set, every vector v from A to B has a corresponding vector -v from -A to -B. So, if you have a pair AB with vector v, then the pair (-A)(-B) has vector -v, which is parallel to v (since lines are undirected). Therefore, in a centrally symmetric set, every line AB has a parallel line (-A)(-B). So, in this case, if we take a centrally symmetric finite set in 3D, not all coplanar, then it might satisfy the condition.\n\nFor example, take the cube: it's centrally symmetric. So for every pair AB, the pair (-A)(-B) is parallel. But as we saw earlier, in the cube, some lines (the space diagonals) when taken as AB, the pair (-A)(-B) is the same line, because if A is (1,1,1) and B is (-1,-1,-1), then -A is (-1,-1,-1) = B, and -B is (1,1,1) = A. So, the line AB is the same as (-A)(-B). Hence, in this case, the line AB is the same as CD, but the problem states \"t