,000 -1)/(20,000 +1). Let me compute this fraction:\n\n19999/20001 = 1 - 2/20001 \u2248 1 - 0.000099995\n\nTherefore, ln(19999/20001) \u2248 ln(1 - 0.000099995) \u2248 -0.000099995 - (0.000099995)^2/2 - ... \u2248 -0.000099995\n\nMultiply by (n +1) = 10,001:\n\n\u2248 -0.000099995 * 10,001 \u2248 -0.99995\n\nTherefore, exponentiating gives e^{-0.99995} \u2248 0.3679, which is very close to 1/e \u2248 0.367879...\n\nAh, so my previous mistake was miscalculating the exponent. So even for n=10,000, the value is approaching 1/e. So numerical evidence supports our analytical result.\n\nTherefore, through multiple approaches\u2014logarithmic transformation, Taylor series expansion, rewriting in terms of known limits, and numerical verification\u2014it's clear that the limit is 1/e.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{e}}", "deepseek_solution": "To calculate the limit of the numerical sequence:\n$$\\lim _{n \\rightarrow \\infty}\\left(\\frac{2 n-1}{2 n+1}\\right)^{n+1}$$\n\nwe start by recognizing that the base of the exponent, \\(\\frac{2n - 1}{2n + 1}\\), approaches 1 as \\(n\\) approaches infinity, leading to an indeterminate form \\(1^\\infty\\). We use the natural logarithm to transform the limit into a more manageable form.\n\nLet \\(L\\) be the limit we seek. Taking the natural logarithm of both sides, we have:\n\\[\n\\ln L = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} (n + 1) \\ln\\left(\\frac{2n - 1}{2n + 1}\\right)\n\\]\n\nWe rewrite the fraction inside the logarithm:\n\\[\n\\frac{2n - 1}{2n + 1} = 1 - \\frac{2}{2n + 1}\n\\]\n\nFor large \\(n\\), \\(\\frac{2}{2n + 1}\\) is small, so we use the approximation \\(\\ln(1 - x) \\approx -x\\) for small \\(x\\):\n\\[\n\\ln\\left(1 - \\frac{2}{2n + 1}\\right) \\approx -\\frac{2}{2n + 1}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting this approximation into the limit, we get:\n\\[\n\\ln L \\approx \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} (n + 1) \\left(-\\frac{2}{2n + 1}\\right)\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the expression inside the limit:\n\\[\n\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} -\\frac{2(n + 1)}{2n + 1} = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} -\\frac{2n + 2}{2n + 1} = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} -\\frac{2 + \\frac{2}{n}}{2 + \\frac{1}{n}} = -1\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(\\ln L = -1\\), which implies \\(L = e^{-1}\\).\n\nTo confirm, we consider the original expression in terms of exponentials:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{2n - 1}{2n + 1}\\right)^{n+1} = \\left(\\frac{1 - \\frac{1}{2n}}{1 + \\frac{1}{2n}}\\right)^{n+1}\n\\]\n\nFor large \\(n\\), \\(\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2n}\\right)^n \\approx e^{-1/2}\\) and \\(\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{2n}\\right)^n \\approx e^{1/2}\\). The ratio of these terms raised to the power \\(n+1\\) approaches \\(e^{-1}\\).\n\nNumerical verification for large \\(n\\) also supports this result. Therefore, the limit is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{e}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "To calculate the limit of the sequence \n\\[\n\\lim_{n \\rightarrow \\infty} \\left( \\frac{2n-1}{2n+1} \\right)^{n+1}\n\\]\n\n1. **Manipulate the fraction inside the limit:**\n\n   Rewrite the original expression:\n   \\[\n   \\lim_{n \\rightarrow \\infty} \\left( \\frac{2n-1}{2n+1} \\right)^{n+1}\n   \\]\n   \n   Express the fraction as the reciprocal:\n   \\[\n   \\lim_{n \\rightarrow \\infty} \\left( \\frac{2n+1}{2n-1} \\right)^{-(n+1)}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Simplify the fraction:**\n\n   Add and subtract 2 in the numerator of the fraction to express it differently:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2n+1}{2n-1} = \\frac{2n-1 + 2}{2n-1} = 1 + \\frac{2}{2n-1}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus, the expression becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\lim_{n \\rightarrow \\infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{2}{2n-1}\\right)^{-(n+1)}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Introduce substitution to recognize the form of the exponential limit:**\n\n   Let \\( a_n = \\frac{2}{2n-1} \\). The expression now is:\n   \\[\n   \\lim_{n \\rightarrow \\infty} \\left(1 + a_n\\right)^{-\\frac{2n-1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{2(n+1)}{2n-1}}\n   \\]\n   \n   Rewrite it into the form that makes it easier to recognize the exponential limit:\n   \\[\n   \\lim_{n \\rightarrow \\infty} \\left(1 + \\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{2n-1}{2}\\right)} \\right)^{\\left(\\frac{2n-1}{2}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{-2(n+1)}{2n-1}\\right)}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Recognize the exponential form:**\n\n   Notice that this can be rewritten using the exponential limit identity \\( e^x = \\lim_{m \\rightarrow \\infty} \\left( 1 + \\frac{1}{m} \\right)^m \\):\n   \\[\n   \\lim_{n \\rightarrow \\infty}\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{\\frac{2n-1}{2}}\\right)^{\\left(\\frac{2n-1}{2}\\right)} = e\n   \\]\n   \n   Therefore, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\lim_{n \\rightarrow \\infty} \\left( 1 + \\frac{1}{\\frac{2n-1}{2}} \\right)^{\\frac{2n - 1}{2}} \\right)^{\\lim_{n \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{-2(n+1)}{2n-1}}\n   = e^{\\lim_{n \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{-2(n+1)}{2n-1}}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Simplify the exponent:**\n\n   Now simplify the exponent:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{-2(n+1)}{2n-1} = \\frac{-(2n+2)}{2n-1}\n   \\]\n\n   To simplify this, divide numerator and denominator by \\( n \\):\n   \\[\n   \\lim_{n \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{\\frac{-(2n+2)}{n}}{\\frac{2n-1}{n}} = \\lim_{n \\rightarrow \\infty} \\frac{-2 - \\frac{2}{n}}{2 - \\frac{1}{n}} = \\frac{-2 - 0}{2 - 0} = -1\n   \\]\n\n   Thus, the exponent simplifies to \\( -1 \\):\n\n6. **Calculate the final value:**\n\n   Substitute the simplified exponent back into the expression:\n   \\[\n   e^{-1} = \\frac{1}{e}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe limit of the sequence is\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{e}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find all functions from the set of positive integers to the set of positive integers such that:\n\n$$\nm^{2} + f(n) \\mid m f(m) + n\n$$\n\nfor all \\( m, n > 0 \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find all functions f from the positive integers to the positive integers such that for every pair of positive integers m and n, the expression m\u00b2 + f(n) divides m f(m) + n. Hmm, that seems a bit abstract. Let me start by understanding what the problem is asking for.\n\nFirst,